## Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA)

## Structure

```typescript
test('adds two numbers', () => {
  // ARRANGE — set up
  const calculator = new Calculator();

  // ACT — invoke the behaviour
  const result = calculator.add(2, 3);

  // ASSERT — verify the outcome
  expect(result).toBe(5);
});
```

## Benefits

- **Readability**: immediately clear what's being tested
- **Single responsibility**: one Act per test = one thing being tested
- **Debugging**: when a test fails, the three sections tell you exactly where to look

## React Component Example

```typescript
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import Counter from './Counter';

test('increments count when button is clicked', async () => {
  // ARRANGE
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<Counter initialCount={0} />);

  // ACT
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /increment/i }));

  // ASSERT
  expect(screen.getByText('Count: 1')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

## Async Example

```typescript
test('fetches and displays user name', async () => {
  // ARRANGE
  jest.spyOn(api, 'getUser').mockResolvedValue({ name: 'Aaron' });
  render(<UserProfile userId="1" />);

  // ACT (implicit — fetch triggered on mount)

  // ASSERT
  expect(await screen.findByText('Aaron')).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```

## Variation: Given-When-Then

BDD style — same concept, different names:
- **Given** (Arrange)
- **When** (Act)
- **Then** (Assert)

## Unit Tests vs Integration Tests

## Unit Tests

```typescript
// Testing formatPrice in isolation
test('formats price with currency symbol', () => {
  expect(formatPrice(9.99, 'GBP')).toBe('£9.99');
  expect(formatPrice(9.99, 'USD')).toBe('$9.99');
});

// Dependencies are mocked — no DB, no network
test('getUserById returns user when found', async () => {
  const mockDb = { findOne: jest.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: '1', name: 'Aaron' }) };
  const result = await getUserById('1', mockDb);
  expect(result.name).toBe('Aaron');
});
```

**Pros**: Fast, precise, isolated, great for logic
**Cons**: Don't test how units connect

## Integration Tests

```typescript
// Testing the real API + real database (or test DB)
test('POST /api/users creates a user and returns 201', async () => {
  const res = await request(app)
    .post('/api/users')
    .send({ name: 'Aaron', email: 'aaron@example.com' });

  expect(res.status).toBe(201);
  expect(res.body.id).toBeDefined();

  // Verify it's actually in the database
  const user = await db.user.findUnique({ where: { email: 'aaron@example.com' } });
  expect(user).not.toBeNull();
});
```

**Pros**: Catch real-world interface bugs, test actual behaviour
**Cons**: Slower, need DB/infrastructure, harder to debug

## The Value of Each

```
Unit tests catch: Logic bugs, edge cases, algorithm errors
Integration tests catch:
  - Wrong SQL queries
  - Misconfigured middleware
  - Data transformation bugs between layers
  - Auth/permission bugs
  - API contract mismatches
```

## Recommended Split

70% Unit + 20% Integration + 10% E2E (the "testing trophy" for frontend differs: more integration)

## Benefits of Unit Testing

## 1. Refactoring Confidence

The biggest practical benefit: you can change the implementation without worrying about breaking behaviour — the tests catch regressions immediately.

```typescript
// Before: formatDate uses manual string ops
function formatDate(date: Date): string { /* ... */ }

// After: switch to date-fns internally
// Tests still pass → behaviour unchanged
// No manual testing needed
```

## 2. Living Documentation

Tests describe what the code does from the caller's perspective:

```typescript
describe('calculateDiscount', () => {
  it('applies 10% for standard members', () => { ... });
  it('applies 20% for premium members', () => { ... });
  it('caps discount at £50', () => { ... });
  it('throws for negative price', () => { ... });
});
// Reading this = understanding the function's contract
```

## 3. Early Bug Detection

```
Cost to fix a bug:
  During development (caught by unit test): £1
  During QA: £10
  In production: £100-£1000
```

## 4. Better Design

Writing tests forces you to think about API design:
- Hard-to-test code → tightly coupled, unclear responsibilities
- Easy-to-test code → injected dependencies, single responsibility, pure functions

## 5. Onboarding Safety Net

New developers can make changes without fear of silently breaking things — the test suite provides a guardrail.

## 6. CI/CD Enablement

Fast, reliable unit tests are what make it safe to deploy many times per day.

## Testing Getters and Setters

## When NOT to Test (trivial getter/setter)

```typescript
class User {
  private _name: string;

  get name() { return this._name; }       // trivial — no logic
  set name(v: string) { this._name = v; } // trivial — no logic
}

// ❌ This test has no value:
test('name getter returns name', () => {
  const user = new User();
  user.name = 'Aaron';
  expect(user.name).toBe('Aaron'); // tests the language, not your code
});
```

## When TO Test (getter/setter with logic)

```typescript
class Product {
  private _price: number = 0;

  get formattedPrice(): string {
    return `£${this._price.toFixed(2)}`; // has logic
  }

  set price(value: number) {
    if (value < 0) throw new Error('Price must be non-negative');
    this._price = Math.round(value * 100) / 100; // normalises to 2 decimal places
  }
}

// ✅ These tests have value:
test('formattedPrice formats with £ symbol and 2 decimals', () => {
  const p = new Product();
  p.price = 9.9;
  expect(p.formattedPrice).toBe('£9.90');
});

test('price setter rejects negative values', () => {
  const p = new Product();
  expect(() => { p.price = -5; }).toThrow('Price must be non-negative');
});
```

## The Principle

**Test behaviour, not implementation.** A getter/setter is an implementation detail. What matters is the observable behaviour of the object — if the object correctly prices products, filters lists, and raises errors. Whether it uses a getter internally is irrelevant to the test.
